Consider the octahedron as having two sets of four faces each, with opposite faces in each set being the faces of a square. Then, we can assign pairs of consecutive numbers to the faces of the squares. There are two ways to do this: either $(1,2)$, $(3,4)$, $(5,6)$, and $(7,8)$, or $(1,3)$, $(2,4)$, $(5,7)$, and $(6,8)$. 

Next, we need to consider the arrangement of the remaining number, $9$, on the octahedron. There are three ways to place $9$ on the octahedron, as shown in the diagram below:
[asy]
draw(circle((0,0),1));
draw((0,0)--dir(45));
draw((0,0)--dir(135));
draw((0,0)--dir(225));
draw((0,0)--dir(315));
draw((0,0)--(1,0));
draw((0,0)--(sqrt(3)/2,1/2));
draw((0,0)--(-sqrt(3)/2,1/2));
draw((0,0)--(-1,0));
draw((0,0)--(-sqrt(3)/2,-1/2));
draw((0,0)--(sqrt(3)/2,-1/2));
draw((0,0)--(1/2,-sqrt(3)/2));
draw((0,0)--(3/2,-sqrt(3)/2));
draw((0,0)--(1/2,sqrt(3)/2));
draw((0,0)--(3/2,sqrt(3)/2));
[/asy]
Each of these arrangements can be rotated into $48$ distinct configurations. Thus, there are $2 \cdot 3 \cdot 48 = 288$ desirable arrangements. Since there are $8! = 40320$ total arrangements, the probability that no two consecutive numbers are written on faces that share an edge is $288/40320 = 1/140$. Therefore, $m + n = 1 + 140 = 141$.</think> <answer>141</answer>

